---
term: Associative consolidation
aka:
  - Consolidation
  - Deconsolidation
  - Membership tier
  - Tier
section: life-cycle
order: 52
definition: A party's movement between discrete tiers that a charter defines, from base [[associate]] to full [[member]], in either direction.
seeAlso:
  - status
  - admission
  - centralization
---
A charter's articles are discrete provisions written in language, so the terms of belonging are discrete too. Each tier is a set of articles that the party accepts. A charter can define any number of tiers between base associate and full member, or none.

Consolidation applies to people and organizations alike, because an individual takes part as a [[personal corporation]]. At the shallow end, a person takes a single paid task. At the deep end, they hold every right and obligation the charter defines. Between charters, the same range runs from a supplier contract to a member subsidiary.

A party can consolidate toward full membership or deconsolidate back toward associate status. Which dimensions a tier varies, such as voice, protection, exit cost, or commitment, and how a party moves between tiers are choices for each charter's design. The framework sets no limit on how far a party can consolidate.

Consolidation matters most for specialization. Skills that are useful anywhere, which economists call *general* skills, don't need consolidation, because an open market already rewards them. Skills and assets that are valuable only inside one charter, called *specific* investment, expose the party to renegotiation once it has committed them. Persistent membership is the traditional protection. Tiers let a charter offer that protection without requiring every party to become a permanent member.
